Format, write, wipe and inspect USB devices
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Every command that changes the device prints itself with a short explanation
and asks before it runs.

THREE MODES
  usb format /dev/sdb --fs vfat       show + ask + run
  usb -s format /dev/sdb --fs vfat    show only, nothing runs
  usb -y format /dev/sdb --fs vfat    run without asking

  -s and -y are global LPEX flags. They may appear up to the first extension
  value, so 'usb -s format /dev/sdb' and 'usb format -s /dev/sdb' both work,
  while 'usb format /dev/sdb -s' does not.

  Note: -s only suppresses commands marked with --confirm. Read-only lookups
  keep running, otherwise the following steps would lose their input values.

RECIPES
  format   set up a stick from scratch
           umount -> wipefs -> partition table -> partition -> mkfs -> verify
  write    write an ISO/IMG (dd) and compare it back byte by byte
  wipe     erase everything, including hidden and leftover partitions
           wipefs plus zeroing start AND end of the device (GPT backup!)
  check    read-only: partitions, filesystems, labels, UUIDs, vendor

SAFETY
  No recipe touches a disk carrying /, /boot, /home, /usr, /var or swap.
  That block cannot be bypassed, not even with -y.
  Destructive recipes require the device name to be typed out in full.

WHICH FILESYSTEM?
  vfat   FAT32  readable everywhere, but no single file above 4 GB
  exfat         large files, Windows/macOS/Linux — needs exfatprogs
  ext4          Linux only, but real permissions and a journal
